(ARG-13 — `the argument at the <number variable>` reads an argument by a
 computed index (LANGUAGE.md:4764-4769). The manual's own example uses
 index 2 and calls the result the second user argument, which is what
 index 2 returns here.
 Also covers: ARG-44 — the indexing is over argv, not over the user
 arguments: index 0 is the program name (no error), index 1 is the first
 user argument, a negative index sets the error flag and yields "", and
 both a bare literal index and a computed expression are accepted where
 the manual shows only a variable.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox ARG-13.vox -o p && ./p alpha beta gamma
 Args: alpha beta gamma
 expected output:
   at two is beta
   index 0 is the program name
   at one is alpha
   a negative index errored
   at backwards is []
   at literal is gamma
   at expression is beta
)
a number called secondth is 2.
a text called 'at two' is the argument at the secondth.
Print "at two is {'at two'}".
a number called nothingth is 0.
a text called 'at nothing' is the argument at the nothingth.
On error print "index 0 errored".
If 'at nothing' is arguments's name then,
    Print "index 0 is the program name".
a number called firstth is 1.
a text called 'at one' is the argument at the firstth.
Print "at one is {'at one'}".
a number called backwards is 0 subtract 1.
a text called 'at backwards' is the argument at the backwards.
On error print "a negative index errored".
Print "at backwards is [{'at backwards'}]".
a text called 'at literal' is the argument at 3.
Print "at literal is {'at literal'}".
a text called 'at expression' is the argument at the firstth add 1.
Print "at expression is {'at expression'}".
